```
void main() {
    function_taking_span({ 1, 2, 3});
}
```
Is this creating an array of `ints` on the stack and then passing that into the function? 

Also, would the compiler try to implicitly convert types here? Like if `function_taking_span()` was defined with `const Span<bool> span` instead, what would happen?

> ```
> void main() {
>     function_taking_span({ 1, 2, 3});
> }
> ```
> 
> Is this creating an array of `ints` on the stack and then passing that into the function?

I [double checked](https://godbolt.org/z/dfcGrn1dz) to be sure and indeed, it's not allocating any heap space. It's like a transient in-line array.

> Also, would the compiler try to implicitly convert types here? Like if `function_taking_span()` was defined with `const Span<bool> span` instead, what would happen?

Yep! I tested this; implicit conversions are happening if necessitated by the code.

I don't think it's a good idea to implement such a constructor. It's very dangerous and easy to miss the fact that a span is not a container and does not own the underlying buffer. This constructor saves a reference to a temporary object, so even if very experienced C++ developers can avoid that trap, it remains a trap which can be very hard to debug.

Here is an example:
```
void process(const Span<const int>& span)
{
    std::cout << span[0] << std::endl;
}

Span<const int> make_span()
{
    return {1, 2, 3, 4};
}

int main() 
{
    process(make_span());
    return 0;
}
```
That looks good at first sight because we only work with temporaries, but gcc prints 1 while clang prints 0...

Moreover, I think the current implementation has UB because there is no constraint on the constness of `T`. C++26 added this constructor in `std::span` with a `requires (std::is_const_v<T>)` to enforce the lifetime extension of the `std::initializer_list`. 

Also gcc produce warnings which must be disabled (-Winit-list-lifetime).

I experimented a bit if there's an alternative way to construct a `Span` in-place to pass to the function.

It's also possible using `std::array`:

<details>
<summary>Implementation</summary>

```c++
    template <size_t N>
    constexpr Span(const std::array<const T&, N> &p_array) {
         _ptr = p_array.data();
         if constexpr (is_string) {
             _len = N;
             // Cut off the \0 terminator implicitly added to string literals.
             if (N > 0 && p_array[N - 1] == '\0') {
                 _len--;
             }
         }
         else {
             _len = N;
         }
     }

    template <size_t N>
    constexpr Span(const std::array<T, N> &p_array) {
         _ptr = p_array.data();
         if constexpr (is_string) {
             _len = N;
             // Cut off the \0 terminator implicitly added to string literals.
             if (N > 0 && p_array[N - 1] == '\0') {
                 _len--;
             }
         }
         else {
             _len = N;
         }
     }
```
</details>

Calls are less pretty (but work):
```c++
    function_taking_span(std::array { 'a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e' });
    char a = 'a', b = 'b', c = 'c';
    function_taking_span(std::array { a, b, c });
```

But more importantly, the same limitation applies as with `initializer_list`:
```c++
Span<int> make_span()
{
    return std::array {1, 2, 3, 4};  // Compiles, but is technically UB!
}
```

Thinking about it further, the same limitation applies to pretty much all constructors of `Span`:

```c++
Span<int> make_span()
{
    int a = 5;
    return a;  // Compiles, but is technically UB!
}
```
(https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/103931)

```c++
Span<char> make_span()
{
    char a[] = { a, b, c };
    return a;  // Compiles, but is technically UB!
}
```
(https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/103923)

```c++
Span<char32_t> make_span()
{
    String result = make_string();
    return result;  // Compiles, but is technically UB!
}
```
(current master)

I think this is just something we'll have to live with for `Span`. 
This PR may be a bit more 'inviting' to just return an array as `Span`, but it's an inherent risk to `Span` in general. You just have to treat it like a pointer, and returning pointers to stack variables is illegal. In general, `Span` as a return value is probably not a great idea unless it's clear that it points to memory owned by the callee.
